.TH SRIOV\-MAC\-SYNC 8 "August 2026" "sriov\-mac\-sync" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sriov\-mac\-sync \- keep an SR\-IOV uplink's unicast filter in step with the bridge behind it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sriov\-mac\-sync
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
A network card in SR\-IOV mode forwards between its physical function, its
virtual functions and the wire with an internal switch \(em a VEB. That switch
does not learn: it delivers to the addresses it has been told about, which are
the ones the driver programmed for each function and nothing else.
.PP
A host that puts a bridge behind such a card breaks that assumption. Containers,
other guests, the host's own address, a printer on a second card \(em all of them
live behind the bridge, and the VEB has never heard of any of them. A frame from
a virtual function to one of those addresses is a miss, and a miss leaves through
the physical port. The frame goes out on the wire, addressed to something that is
not out there, and nothing is logged.
.PP
The signature is unmistakable once you know it: ARP or ND resolves, because that
is broadcast and gets flooded, while the unicast reply never arrives.
.PP
.B sriov\-mac\-sync
watches the bridge's forwarding database over rtnetlink and writes what the
bridge has learnt into the uplink's unicast filter, adding addresses as they
appear and removing them as the bridge ages them out \(em unless the port an
address was learnt behind still exists in the bridge: such a device is merely
quiet and stays registered while its port lives, and when the filter nears
its capacity the longest\-missing entries are released first.
That memory is the daemon's own; a hand-run \fB\-\-once\fR beside it, or a
restart, falls back to plain ageing until the next ARP. It works out which
interface is the uplink structurally \(em an interface with virtual functions, or
one that is a virtual function \(em following bonds and stacked bridges, so a card
sitting under a VLAN\-aware bridge or an SDN vnet is found too.
.PP
It records what it registered, in
.IR /run/sriov\-mac\-sync ,
and removes only its own entries. Addresses that belong out on the wire \(em the
uplink's own, its siblings', a bond member's \(em are never registered, and one
that moves out onto the wire is taken back out.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B (no option)
Run as a daemon.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Reconcile once and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Show what is detected, wanted and registered.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Test whether the uplink accepts unicast filter entries at all. This only proves
that the kernel took the entry; whether the card acts on it is a property of the
driver and cannot be asked. See
.B VERIFYING
below.
.TP
.B \-\-flush
Remove every address this daemon registered. A plain stop deliberately leaves
them in place, because they are what makes the peers reachable.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report the changes without applying them.
.TP
.B \-\-timings
After every pass, say what each phase cost and what it found, and name anything
that failed along the way.
.TP
.BI \-\-pair " DEV:BR"
Uplink and bridge to manage. Repeatable, and skips autodetection entirely.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " SEC"
Seconds between full reconciliations (default 300). This is a safety net, not
the mechanism: addresses are registered the moment the kernel reports them.
.TP
.BI \-\-max " NUM"
Warn above this many addresses on one uplink (default 128). The vport list on a
ConnectX\-4 Lx holds 128; other hardware differs. Where the card reports its
capacity through the devlink parameter
.B max_macs
the daemon uses that instead, and this option overrides both.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " MACS"
Addresses never to register, separated by commas or whitespace.
.TP
.BI \-\-extra " MACS"
Addresses to register unconditionally, for a device that never speaks first. The
wire\-side and own\-address rules still override this.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Explain what is skipped and why.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/sriov\-mac\-sync.conf
is read at startup if it exists. Every setting is optional and corresponds to an
option above: shell\-style
.IR KEY=VALUE ,
one per line, with
.B #
starting a comment.
.TP
.B PAIRS
Space\-separated
.I DEV:BRIDGE
pairs. Empty means autodetect.
.TP
.B RESYNC
Seconds between full reconciliations.
.TP
.B MAX_MACS
Warning threshold per uplink.
.TP
.B EXCLUDE
Addresses never to register. \fB\-\-exclude\fR on the command line adds to
this list rather than replacing it.
.TP
.B EXTRA
Addresses to register unconditionally. \fB\-\-extra\fR likewise adds to it.
.SH VERIFYING
That the kernel accepted an entry is not the same as the card acting on it.
Test with traffic, in this order:
.IP 1. 4
From inside the virtual function's guest, ping something behind the bridge. It
should fail.
.IP 2. 4
.B bridge fdb add
.I <that mac>
.B dev
.I <uplink>
.B self permanent
.IP 3. 4
Ping again. It should work.
.IP 4. 4
.B bridge fdb del
the same entry. It should fail again.
.PP
If step 3 changes nothing, this approach does not work on that hardware and this
daemon will not help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sriov\-mac\-sync.conf
Optional configuration.
.TP
.I /run/sriov\-mac\-sync/
One note per uplink, recording which entries are this daemon's - written before
the entry is, so a crash cannot leave an entry nobody owns. Beside each note, a
record of the uplink's interface index, so the note can follow a renamed
interface. Kept across a restart on purpose; a reboot clears it, which is right,
because the card's filter is empty by then too.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/sriov\-mac\-sync.service
The service, as installed by the Debian package.
.TP
.I /etc/init.d/sriov\-mac\-sync
The procd service, as installed by the OpenWrt package.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success. Non\-zero when a requested action failed \(em an uplink that could not
be found, a filter the kernel refused, a configuration that named nothing.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
stop the daemon and leave the registrations in the card. Use
.B \-\-flush
to take them back.
.SH EXAMPLES
Before starting anything, ask whether this hardware plays along at all:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-check
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-once \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run it against one explicit pair, without autodetection:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-pair nic1v0:vmbr1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ask a running installation what it thinks the world looks like:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sriov\-mac\-sync \-\-status
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITS
The unicast list is finite; beyond it the driver
drops addresses silently. Hardware that can represent virtual functions as real
bridge ports \(em switchdev with bridge offload \(em should use that instead; this
exists for the cards that cannot.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bridge (8),
.BR ip\-link (8),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
